---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-the-uk`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in the UK',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in the UK', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in the UK: PHE Guidance, Winter Deficiency, and NHS Recommendations"
  description="UK-specific vitamin D — Public Health England guidance (10 µg / 400 IU year-round), why nobody makes vitamin D Oct–Mar, and NHS recommendations by group."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-the-uk", label: "Vitamin D in the UK" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in the UK</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      The United Kingdom sits between 50° and 60° north — high enough that from
      October through March, essentially no vitamin D is made from sunlight anywhere
      in the country. Combined with famous cloud cover, this makes the UK one of
      the world's paradigmatic vitamin D-deficient developed nations. Public Health
      England (now UKHSA) recommends year-round supplementation for the whole
      population.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">PHE / UKHSA guidance (2016, unchanged)</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>All adults and children over 1:</strong> 10 µg (400 IU) per day, particularly autumn and winter.</li>
      <li><strong>Infants under 1 year, breastfed or receiving &lt; 500 mL formula/day:</strong> 8.5–10 µg per day year-round.</li>
      <li><strong>Bottle-fed infants receiving 500+ mL formula/day:</strong> no supplement needed (formula is fortified).</li>
      <li><strong>People at increased risk</strong> (dark skin, limited sun, covered clothing, housebound): 10 µg year-round.</li>
      <li>Free NHS supplements available through Healthy Start scheme for eligible pregnant women and children.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">UK-specific deficiency data</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      NDNS (National Diet and Nutrition Survey) findings: 20–30% of UK adults are
      deficient (25(OH)D &lt; 25 nmol/L) at some point annually, with peak winter
      deficiency rates 35–50%. Higher rates in older adults, South Asian and Black
      British populations, and residents of northern England / Scotland.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When can you make vitamin D in the UK?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Roughly April through September, and only around midday. Sun exposure for
      ~10–30 minutes to arms and face several times a week during those months
      provides useful synthesis for fair-skinned adults. Winter sun in the UK does
      not produce meaningful vitamin D at any time of day — regardless of how
      pleasant it feels.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buying vitamin D in the UK</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>OTC in Boots, Superdrug, supermarkets, and pharmacies.</li>
      <li>Standard adult dose 10 µg (400 IU) supplements ~£3–5 for a year's supply.</li>
      <li>Higher-strength 25 µg (1,000 IU) and 50 µg (2,000 IU) available OTC — reasonable for winter supplementation especially in the deficient.</li>
      <li>Prescription-strength 20,000 IU or 50,000 IU capsules are available with GP referral for correction of documented deficiency.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Testing 25(OH)D in the UK</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      NHS GPs can order 25(OH)D testing but often decline unless there's a clinical
      indication (bone pain, malabsorption, chronic disease, unexplained symptoms).
      Private testing is available for £30–60 (Medichecks, Thriva, Better You home
      finger-prick tests). UK 25(OH)D thresholds:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Deficient: &lt; 25 nmol/L (10 ng/mL) — NHS action level.</li>
      <li>Insufficient: 25–50 nmol/L (10–20 ng/mL).</li>
      <li>Adequate: &gt; 50 nmol/L (&gt; 20 ng/mL).</li>
      <li>Note: US thresholds use ng/mL and a higher target — 30+ ng/mL. See our <a href="/vitamin-d-unit-converter" class="text-amber-700 underline">unit converter</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> UK winter is not a debate about whether
      to supplement — take a 10–25 µg cholecalciferol pill daily October through
      March at minimum.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-the-uk" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
